[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Creating an AI persona is as easy as writing a system prompt. You can add as much or as little detail as you want, and you can tweak it over time until you get it just right. Uh but, as soon as you shut down that session, the LLM forgets everything. And when you start it back up again later, the persona is seeing you as if for the first time.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s forgotten everything that you\'ve talked about. Wouldn\'t it be nice if we could get the persona to remember things that we\'ve told it, so that when you start it back up again later, the conversation flows a little more naturally? Can we do it? You know what? I\'ll bet we can. Let\'s try it.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Okay, if you\'re new here, one of the things we\'ve been doing on this channel is slowly building up an AI group chat application. It looks like this. Talk'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with me. Uh version six coming soon. The idea is you can set up and configure multiple AI personas uh and then add them to a chat room and interact with them. Each persona has got an image and an AI'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cloned voice and a system prompt. The system prompt for each persona is what allows the LLM to kind of get into character and deliver responses as that character. So, you can get as detail as you want in in the system prompt, and uh it improves the uh realism of the'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'experience. Now, up to this point, uh I\'ve been using Star Trek characters as the examples uh for this application. But, it\'s not tied to Star Trek by any means. You can uh you can add whoever you like. So, before we dive into this episode\'s'

